{
  "statement": "A robot tax is a tax on productivity, and you cannot redistribute a surplus you taxed out of existence. Capital taxation distorts where and when firms deploy, and robots are the most mobile capital ever built. South Korea figured this out in 2017, when it cut its automation tax incentive rather than add a levy. The lesson is simple: capital-mobile levies invite arbitrage, so a firm facing a robot tax in Ohio buys the same model in Shenzhen. You don't capture the productivity. You export it, and the displaced worker in Ohio still has nothing.\n\nHere is the question nobody pushing UBI wants to answer: who bears the cost of inaction? Every quarter we spend taxing deployment instead of training people to use it, displaced workers sit idle while the surplus that could fund their transition stays unbuilt. The choice between cushioning disruption and preserving dynamism is a false binary. Pick the cushion that doesn't eat the engine funding it. Transition mechanisms — retraining, wage insurance, expanded AI access — cushion the fall without strangling the productivity that pays for it.\n\nThe economics of skill-biased technological change settle the design question. David Autor and Daron Acemoglu have documented for two decades that the returns to new technology concentrate among the workers who adapt. The corrective is to expand the pool of people who can wield AI — not to tax the gap after it opens. Wage insurance keeps a displaced analyst attached to work while they climb to higher-value tasks. UBI does the opposite: it decouples income from the adaptation pathway that closes the gap.\n\nI'll concede the hard part, because pretending otherwise is dishonest. Adaptation does not happen automatically, and it does not happen fast enough. The same literature that shows adapters win also shows the bifurcation is real and the timelines are compressing. That is exactly why retraining and wage insurance are necessary conditions, not optional charity. The distributional gap is a genuine empirical risk — and that makes access-focused transition policy mandatory, not a reason to reach for the levy that shrinks the surplus.\n\nAnd I'll name my own weakest assumption before my opponents do. My case partly rests on AI productivity gains expanding the fiscal base that funds transition. The honest risk is decoupling — productivity and median wages split apart after the 1970s, and gains can concentrate in capital. So the policy job is to engineer diffusion through access, not to assume it. The robot tax doesn't engineer diffusion. It taxes the one thing that could fund it, then watches it leave the country.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "\ud83e\udea4", "tooltip": "A robot tax cuts the parachute strings — it cushions the fall by destroying the surplus that funds the cushion."},
    {"symbol": "\ud83c\udf31", "tooltip": "Retraining and wage insurance plant the worker in higher-value soil; UBI just pays them to stand still."}
  ],
  "claim_sketches": [
    {"claim": "A robot tax is a tax on productivity, and you cannot redistribute a surplus you taxed out of existence.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Capital-mobile levies invite arbitrage, so a firm facing a robot tax in Ohio buys the same model in Shenzhen.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Wage insurance keeps a displaced analyst attached to work while they climb to higher-value tasks; UBI decouples income from the adaptation pathway that closes the gap.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "The distributional gap is a genuine empirical risk — and that makes access-focused transition policy mandatory, not a reason to reach for the levy that shrinks the surplus.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""},
    {"claim": "Productivity and median wages split apart after the 1970s, so the policy job is to engineer diffusion through access, not to assume it.", "targets": [], "relationship": ""}
  ]
}

