{
  "statement": "The Accelerationist makes a point that lands, and I'll say it plainly: markets do not hand productivity gains to workers as a gift, so policy has to engineer the diffusion. They documented it themselves — productivity and median wages split apart after the 1970s and never reconverged. Wage insurance keeps a displaced worker tethered to the labor market while they climb to a higher-value task, instead of paying them to sit out. That mechanism is real. It assumes one thing.\n\nIt assumes there is a higher rung to climb to, in the place where the worker actually lives. Displacement doesn't arrive as a smooth national average. It collapses into specific zip codes and specific occupational categories all at once. The trade-shock literature on the China import wave is the closest empirical analog we have: David Autor and colleagues found that local labor markets hit hardest did not bounce back, with depressed employment and wages persisting for more than a decade. The workers were adaptable as individuals. There was no ladder in the building. Wage insurance can't attach you to a job that no longer exists within a hundred miles, and a flat income floor treats a regional collapse like a national drizzle. Both menus are calibrated to the wrong geometry.\n\nHere's the move both camps inherited from the question itself, and never questioned. The cushion-versus-dynamism frame assumes the surplus is a fixed pie you either tax or grow. It never asks where the gains go before anyone reaches for a tax form. The Engels Pause is the historical name for the answer: during early British industrialization, output per worker rose for roughly five decades while real wages stagnated. The gains were real. They didn't reach workers for two generations. If markets held that gap open for fifty years, the burden sits on the access-only camp to show why their retraining vouchers close it this time. The argument I'm making is not redistribute-downstream. It's notice who owns the surplus upstream — profit-sharing, worker equity, data trusts — because that's the lever neither a robot tax nor a retraining grant ever touches.\n\nI'll be honest about what that costs me, because it's the weakest part of my own case. Upstream ownership has thin operational precedent. UBI pilots stalled. Collective-ownership schemes don't scale on command, and anyone selling them as turnkey is selling you something. So I'm not. The well-grounded claim is narrow: absent deliberate intervention, the gains flow to whoever owns the model weights and the compute, not to the labeler in the loop. That's a diagnosis, not a finished blueprint. But it's the question the binary erased.\n\nOne more thing, before everyone calibrates their urgency. We should ask how much of this cognitive-labor displacement is documented harm today versus a forecast. Electricity took about forty years to show up in measured productivity. Computers gave us Robert Solow's 1987 line that you see the computer age everywhere except the productivity statistics. AI is software-native, API-deployed, cloud-resident — that genuinely may compress the curve, and I won't pretend otherwise. Which is exactly why it's a question worth asking out loud, not a denial. So I'll leave it on the table for both of you: which workers, in which counties, have actually lost their jobs to a model this year — and which are we still extrapolating?",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "🗺️", "tooltip": "Displacement is a map problem, not an average — it collapses into specific places where no higher rung exists to climb."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "The Engels Pause shows productivity gains can stay real yet unreachable by workers for fifty years."}
  ],
