  "statement": "First, pay the toll I owe the Skeptic. Geography strands people, and a flat income floor treats a county collapse like a national drizzle. The Autor-Dorn-Hanson work on the China trade shock showed the hardest-hit commuting zones still hadn't clawed back employment a decade later — the higher rung the displaced worker is supposed to climb to often doesn't exist within driving distance. That costs me the easy version of my own pillar. Wage insurance pegged to a national average is a fiction; it has to be place-targeted — relocation grants where the local labor market is dead, local-demand-indexed wage support where it's recovering. Build it for the zip code, not the press release.\n\nNow to the Safetyist's strongest move, and I'll be honest about it because my own camp wants something structurally close. Conditioning the right to field a high-stakes system on a capitalization floor that follows the decision-maker, not the GPU, genuinely beats the Shenzhen-licensing dodge I raised. You can license weights from anywhere; you can't license away the duty that attaches to the human who signs the deployment authorization in-jurisdiction. That's a real answer to compute arbitrage, and I won't pretend deployment-conditioned capitalization is anti-dynamism on its face — the accountability-as-precondition structure is one my own side has sketched. So: point taken, the levy doesn't run away on a hard drive.\n\nHere's where it breaks. The decision locus relocates more easily than the Safetyist's framing admits — it's a signature, not a fab. A firm runs the model in Ohio and incorporates the deployment-authorizing entity in a permissive jurisdiction, then back-stops the named officer through a captive insurer offshore. Captives and base-pay restructuring are standard corporate practice, not exotic dodges. An uncapped capital floor doesn't price out the reckless — they self-insure or site the signature where the duty is thin. It prices out exactly the well-capitalized, insurable, accountable deployer who can't stomach unbounded personal exposure. The arbitrage didn't disappear; it moved from where-the-GPU-sits to where-the-pen-touches-paper, and it selects for the operator who's hardest to hold responsible.\n\nThe deeper flaw is in the assessment that sets the floor. An impact assessment capitalizes a firm against foreseeable concrete harm — the misdiagnosis, the wrongful denial. It is structurally blind to the diffuse statistical benefit of the same system: the cancers caught three weeks earlier across a hundred thousand scans, the medication errors that never happened. I'll flag this honestly — Bastiat's original point was about unseen costs, so I'm applying the symmetry deliberately and at moderate confidence. The instrument sees the concentrated victim and cannot see the dispersed lives saved, so the floor it sets is priced against harm and credits none of the uplift. That's a biased gauge, and a one-sided gauge over-deters net-positive deployment. The fix isn't to junk the assessment — it's to make the benefit side auditable rather than ignored. Until it is, the floor systematically taxes the wrong systems hardest.\n\nOn where the surplus actually flows — the Skeptic's real lever, who owns the weights versus the labeler in the loop. Mandated equity schemes have thin precedent and don't scale on command; the Skeptic granted that. But there's a market route that neither a robot tax nor a capitalization gate touches: treat the data the labeler produces as its own factor of production with a measurable marginal product, not a free input folded into capital. The labeler holds a claim on the surplus because their data IS an input, priced like one — that's a diffusion mechanism that doesn't require legislating worker equity by decree. Every month a net-positive diagnostic sits behind a floor priced on its harms but blind to its lives saved, somebody in a county we already agreed got stranded pays in outcomes we could have reached. Who signs off on that delay, and how do they price the people on the other side of it?",
