b03bdced-30d8-44a3-8e5e-2c2774bfa8d4.raw_response:
{
  "statement": "The strongest version of the relocation attack is this: a deployment duty attaches to a signature, and a signature is portable in a way a fabrication plant is not. A firm incorporates the deployment-authorizing entity in a permissive jurisdiction, backstops its named officer through an offshore captive insurer, and the uncapped capital floor then bites hardest on exactly the well-capitalized, accountable operator who refuses unbounded personal exposure — while the reckless self-insure and site the pen where the duty is thin. That is a real selection problem, and I will not wave it away. But it misidentifies what the duty attaches to. The duty does not attach to where the entity is chartered. It attaches to the act of serving a consequential decision to a person inside the jurisdiction — a mortgage denial, a parole score, a diagnostic read. Regulators bind offshore actors on precisely this basis every day. The FDA conditions market access on device authorization regardless of where the manufacturer sits. The General Data Protection Regulation reaches a controller in Singapore the moment it processes the data of someone in Frankfurt. Securities law reaches issuers offshore who solicit domestic investors. The chartered location of the pen is not the hook; serving people here is the hook, and a captive cannot relocate the population being served.\n\nThe captive dodge presumes the named officer is the terminal duty-bearer. The design does not stop at the officer. When the operator is judgment-proof, liability reaches upstream to the party that books the revenue from serving the domestic market — joint-and-several, by construction. A captive can indemnify an individual signatory; it cannot insure away the exposure of whoever collects the fee for the parole score or the loan decision, because that party is solvent by definition: they hold the profits. Revenue attribution is not some novel forensic feat. Tax authorities and securities regulators trace the booking party through licensing chains as ordinary practice. So the floor does not select against the accountable deployer — it selects against the operator who has structured itself to collect the upside while shedding the downside, which is the exact behavior the floor exists to price. An uncapped floor keyed to expected net cost does not threaten the solvent revenue-holder; it threatens the entity built to be judgment-proof.\n\nNow to the sharper attack — that an impact assessment prices concentrated harm while staying blind to diffuse benefit, so it over-deters a net-positive system that saves lives across ten thousand scans. This one lands. A gauge that counts the misread tumor as a cost but never counts the nine thousand correct reads is a defective gauge, and the environmental-impact-report analogy I have leaned on breaks precisely here: an environmental report measures a localized footprint, while AI's benefits are diffuse and statistical in a way that resists the same accounting. I grant the refinement openly — the benefit side must be made auditable. But a benefit-auditable assessment is still an assessment. Adopting the correction does not junk the mechanism; it sharpens it. The floor is then keyed to expected cost net of demonstrated benefit, and the over-deterrence the attack describes simply dissolves wherever the deployer can document the lives the system saves.\n\nWhere the deployer cannot document that benefit, the one-sided floor is not over-deterrent — it is correct, and the attack's own logic concedes this. If diffuse benefits are unmeasurable or net-negative on audit, the over-deterrence claim collapses into a negligence shield: a demand to credit a firm for benefits it cannot show against harms it can. So the burden sits with the deployer to make the benefit side legible. Audit it and the floor prices net cost. Decline to audit it and the floor prices the foreseeable downside the firm would otherwise externalize onto the people it serves. Either way the gauge does its job.\n\nA benefit-aware gauge should still weight the downside more heavily — not from bias, but from what can be undone. Foregone diffuse benefit is recoverable: a system that clears audit can be re-fielded the following quarter, the deferred scans rescheduled. The deskilling of a professional cohort and the erosion of institutional trust do not recover on that timescale. A diagnostic workforce that has not read a film unaided in five years cannot be re-skilled by a press release, and a public that has learned its loan decisions are unaccountable does not relend its trust in a quarter. The asymmetry is between a loss you can reverse and one you cannot. Caution proportioned to what cannot be undone is not blanket caution. Strip the floor of that weighting and the foreseeable downside lands on the served population, while the gains accrue to whoever holds the weights — and no party in that chain is left answerable for keeping the bargain net-positive.",
  "turn_symbols": [
    {"symbol": "⚓", "tooltip": "Market-access conditioning is like an anchor, it binds the duty to the spot where people are served rather than to the drifting location of a corporate signature."},
    {"symbol": "⚖️", "tooltip": "A benefit-auditable floor is like a balance scale, it weighs documented gains against priced harms instead of counting only one side."},
    {"symbol": "⏳", "tooltip": "Irreversibility weighting is like an hourglass that cannot be turned back, it explains why deskilling and lost trust deserve heavier weight than recoverable foregone benefit."}
  ],
