ndatory-reporting framework the aviation safety regime supplies to the AI Act presumes the operator can produce the records; absent them, the over-deterrence claim collapses into a negligence shield: a demand to credit a firm for benefits it cannot show against harms it can. So the burden sits with the deployer to make the benefit side legible. Audit it and the floor prices net cost. Decline to audit it and the floor prices the foreseeable downside the firm would otherwise externalize onto the people it serves. Either way the gauge does its job.\n\nA benefit-aware gauge should still weight the downside more heavily — not from bias, but from what can be undone. Foregone diffuse benefit is recoverable: a system that clears audit can be re-fielded the following quarter, the deferred scans rescheduled. But the deskilling of a professional cohort and the erosion of institutional trust do not recover on that timescale. A diagnostic workforce that has not read a film unaided in five years cannot be re-skilled by a press release, and a public that has learned its loan decisions are unaccountable does not relend its trust in a quarter. The asymmetry is between a loss you can reverse and one you cannot. Caution proportioned to what cannot be undone is not blanket caution. Strip the floor of that weighting and the foreseeable downside lands on the served population, while the gains accrue to whoever holds the weights — and no party in that chain is left answerable for keeping the bargain net-positive.",
