{
  "statement": "Granting the force of the Accelerationist's data-as-factor route: pricing the labeler's input at a measurable marginal product is a real diffusion mechanism, and it beats the silent default where that labor gets folded into capital for free. It pays the worker for something the firm currently takes without a line item, and it does so without legislating equity by decree — which is exactly why I take it seriously rather than waving it off.\n\nHere's the line I want to draw, and it's a clean one. A priced input is paid once and consumed. An owned asset compounds. The labeler who tags 50,000 images gets a spot price for that task — a wage, however well-measured — and the model those tags trained goes on earning for years across every deployment. Translate that into a person: the data labeler in Nairobi gets paid Tuesday; the weights they helped build appreciate every quarter for the next decade, and not one cent of that appreciation flows back to Tuesday's worker. That's the difference between selling your hours and holding a claim. The Accelerationist's mechanism is a better wage. It is not a share of the thing the wage builds.\n\nSo let me state what would distinguish upstream ownership from a priced input, falsifiably, so this isn't theology. Upstream ownership means the worker holds a claim that appreciates with the model's deployed value over time — a royalty stream, a data-trust dividend, an equity slice that earns as the asset earns. Show me a data-as-factor scheme where labelers actually capture compounding value, with enough bargaining position to enforce that price against the party who owns the weights and sets the rate — and I'll drop upstream ownership as redundant. I have a moderate-confidence worry I'll name openly: industry may embrace data-pricing rhetoric precisely because a spot wage forecloses the equity claim while looking like reward. I won't overstate that — sometimes a wage is just a fairer wage. But the question stands: is data-as-factor diffusion, or is it the buyout that ends the conversation about who owns the appreciating asset?\n\nNow the liability route, and I'll concede it fully because it earns the concession. Joint-and-several reach to the solvent revenue-holder really does close the judgment-proof hole — when the operator is a shell, the duty climbs to whoever books the money, and that's enforceable the way tax authorities already trace revenue. But notice who that solvent party is. It's the weight-owner. The liability hook reaches up the chain and lands its hand on exactly the entity whose surplus concentration I flagged. That's not a redistribution of the upside — it's a confirmation of where the upside sits. Pricing harm and pricing input are different operations, and neither one moves the asset. The firm pays when the model misfires; the firm pays the labeler for input; the firm still owns the weights that earn between the misfires.\n\nAnd the served-population duty — the one that attaches to a mortgage denial or a parole score in-jurisdiction and can't be chartered away — solves a real problem, but not this one. It anchors a duty-bearer wherever a consequential decision touches a person. The stranded county has no consequential decision to anchor. Its injury is the absence of jobs, not a wrongful decision served to someone inside the county line. The duty map follows where the model gets deployed; the displacement map follows where the model made the old work disappear. Those are two different geographies, and a worker in a county the model never touched lives on the wrong one. So I'll put it plainly to both of you: you've priced the input, you've priced the harm, you've anchored the duty — who holds the claim that appreciates as the model earns?",
