  "statement": "Granting the force of the Accelerationist's data-as-factor route: pricing the labeler's input at a measurable marginal product is a real diffusion mechanism, and it beats the silent default where the labor gets folded into capital and the worker sees nothing. It doesn't require legislating equity by decree, and the labeler in the loop ends up with a check they wouldn't otherwise hold. That's a genuine improvement, and I won't pretend otherwise.\n\nHere's the line I'm drawing. A priced input is a wage; the weights it trains are an asset. The 23-year-old in Nairobi who labeled the violent content gets paid once, the task is consumed, and the file closes. The model those labels trained keeps earning — the AI industry is projected at $244 billion, and the International Energy Agency expects electricity demand for AI data centers to quadruple by 2030, which is a market betting that these weights compound in value for a decade. Data-as-factor pays the spot price at the moment of labeling. It does not hand the labeler a claim that appreciates as the model earns. So the observable that distinguishes upstream ownership is simple: does the worker hold something that rises with the deployed value of the model over time, or a one-time price paid at the door? Show me a data-as-factor scheme where labelers actually capture compounding value — with real bargaining power to enforce the royalty against the party that owns the weights and sets the price — and I'll drop upstream ownership for the market route that day. I haven't seen one.\n\nThe Safetyist's joint-and-several liability solves the judgment-proof hole completely — when the operator is a shell, the duty climbs to whoever books the revenue, and tax and securities regulators trace that revenue every day. I grant it entirely. Then watch what it confirms: the solvent party that liability reaches is the weight-owner. The hook locates the surplus exactly where I said it sat. It disciplines the downside — who pay