  "statement": "The Skeptic's strongest version runs like this: a wage is paid once at the door for a consumed input, while the weights keep earning for a decade, so any one-time data payment leaves the appreciating asset entirely with whoever owns the model — and a labeler who can't enforce a royalty against the price-setter captures none of that compounding value. That's a clean distinction and I'll grant the half of it that's true: a pure spot payment for a static dataset does exactly what he says — it folds the asset into capital and walks the labeler off with cab fare. Where it breaks is the premise that labeling is a one-time consumed input. It isn't. A deployed model that keeps earning needs reinforcement data, drift correction, red-team examples, and continuous fine-tuning — the input is a recurring dependency, not a purchase. And a recurring input priced against deployed usage is, by construction, a claim that rises with the model's earnings. That's not theory: Reddit licensed its data to Google on ongoing terms, not a one-time sale; Shutterstock pays contributors from a recurring royalty pool tied to how its data gets used. High-value data already commands a stream, not a spot price.",
